#Sniffing software, or packet sniffers, are tools that capture and analyze data packets traveling across a network, acting like a digital tap to see all network traffic, revealing source, destination, and content. Used legitimately by network admins for diagnostics and security, they are also used maliciously by hackers to steal sensitive info like passwords or financial data, making them powerful for both system health checks and cyberattacks. 
How they work

    Capture: They monitor network interfaces to intercept data packets, whether intended for the device or not.
    Analyze: They decode protocols and inspect packet headers and payloads to show details like IP addresses, websites visited, email contents, and file downloads.
    Types: Can be software (like Wireshark, tcpdump) or hardware, and use methods like passive monitoring or active techniques (e.g., ARP spoofing) to redirect traffic. 

#Legitimate uses (Network Management)

    Diagnosing network failures and performance issues.
    Auditing configurations and monitoring network health.
    Security analysis to detect threats. 

#Malicious uses (Cyberattacks)

    Capturing login credentials, session tokens, and financial data.
    Espionage and launching further attacks.
    Common attack methods include ARP spoofing (redirecting traffic) and DNS spoofing (redirecting to fake sites). 

#Examples
Network Sniffing
Wireshark - GUI packet analyzer, captures all network traffic
tcpdump - Command-line packet capture for Linux/Unix
Ettercap - MITM attacks and credential sniffing
Kismet - Wireless network detector and sniffer
NetworkMiner - Extracts files/data from network traffic
Burp Suite - Web application traffic interceptor
Fiddler - HTTP/HTTPS debugging and inspection
Wireless/WiFi Sniffing
Aircrack-ng - WiFi security auditing suite (cracking WEP/WPA)
Airodump-ng - Wireless packet capture (part of Aircrack-ng)
Wifite - Automated wireless attack tool
Reaver - WPS PIN attack tool
WiFi Pineapple - Hardware device for WiFi auditing/attacks
Password/Credential Sniffing
Cain & Abel - Windows password recovery and sniffing
John the Ripper - Password cracking tool
Hydra - Network login brute-forcer
Mimikatz - Extracts passwords from Windows memory
Responder - LLMNR/NBT-NS poisoner for credential capture
Keylogging (Keyboard Sniffing)
KeyLogger - Records keystrokes
Spyrix - Commercial keylogger
Actual Keylogger - Monitors keyboard activity
Hardware keyloggers - Physical devices between keyboard and PC
Bluetooth Sniffing
Ubertooth - Bluetooth monitoring hardware/software
Bluesniff - Bluetooth device discovery
BTScanner - Bluetooth device scanner
USB/Hardware Sniffing
USBPcap - USB packet capture for Windows
Wireshark (USB mode) - Can capture USB traffic
Logic analyzers - Hardware for analyzing digital signals
Application/API Sniffing
Postman - API testing and monitoring
Charles Proxy - HTTP proxy for debugging
mitmproxy - Interactive HTTPS proxy
Proxyman - Modern HTTP debugging proxy
Database Sniffing
SQL Injection tools (SQLmap) - Extract database data
MySQL Query Sniffer - Monitors MySQL queries
Mobile Sniffing
Charles Proxy - Mobile app traffic capture
Packet Capture (Android app) - Captures mobile traffic without root
Frida - Dynamic instrumentation for mobile apps
Wireshark (with setup) - Can monitor mobile device traffic
Email Sniffing
MailSniff - Monitors email traffic
Email Header Analyzer - Extracts metadata from emails
Browser Sniffing
Browser Developer Tools - Built-in network inspection (F12)
Cookie editors/viewers - Extract session cookies
Form grabbers - Capture form submissions
Critical Security Note:

Most of these tools are ILLEGAL to use without explicit authorization
Use only on systems/networks you own or have written permission to test
Keyloggers and credential theft tools are particularly sensitive legally
Ethical hackers use these only in authorized penetration testing scenarios
